WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.400
Here's a look at even more of the Dark Phoenix
Easter eggs you may have missed on your first

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.400
watch.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.749
And if you haven't seen the movie, here's
your warning: Spoilers are ahead.

00:00:08.749 --> 00:00:09.849
Did you spot Selene?

00:00:09.849 --> 00:00:13.990
Considering that the X-Men: First Class film
already used the Hellfire Club as antagonists,

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.330
the filmmakers didn't have a whole lot of
options when it came to adapting the Dark

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.960
Phoenix storyline just as it appeared in the
comics especially since the barely-coded sexual

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.430
undertones of the Club in the comics wouldn't
exactly make the group a good fit for a PG-13

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.430
film.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.810
Despite that, they still managed to sneak
in a quick reference to the original Chris

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.010
Claremont and John Byrne comics with one of
Magneto's lieutenants.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.980
One is a guy who throws his hair braids around
as whips and, shockingly, doesn't seem to

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.180
be any one of the hundreds of X-Men characters
featured in the comics.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.160
The other is a telepathic mutant named Selene
although she's only called that the moment

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.780
she's knocked out of the train at the end
of the film.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.730
In the comics, Selene has been a frequent
antagonist to the X-Men and has occasionally

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.020
been the Black Queen of the Hellfire Club.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.810
Comics Selene is also an immortal psychic
vampire who might be the world's very first

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:01.110
mutant, predating Namor, Apocalypse, and Wolverine.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.809
Movie Selene doesn't have much in common with
her comics counterpart, but it's a nice nod

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:05.809
to the canon.

00:01:05.809 --> 00:01:06.809
The Last Class

00:01:06.809 --> 00:01:10.861
While Dark Phoenix is ostensibly an adaptation
of the X-Men story of the same name, it's

00:01:10.861 --> 00:01:14.970
also a conclusion to the new X-Men timeline
that started in X-Men: First Class.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:19.460
As the surviving members of that film, Beast,
Mystique, Professor X, and Magneto all get

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.100
endings to their unique arcs.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:24.620
There's a reason for that: as Beast tells
Mystique during the Dazzler party, "We're

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:25.960
the last of the First Class."

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:30.260
With Darwin killed in the first movie, Angel
and Banshee killed offscreen in Days of Future

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:35.300
Past, and Havok killed in Age of Apocalypse,
only Beast, Mystique, Charles, and Erik are

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.619
around to remember the swinging sixties.

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:41.860
That's also why audiences see Beast in charge
of the Jean Grey school at the end of the

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.270
film; he's the most senior X-Man around who's
not busy playing chess in Parisian cafes.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.790
Magneto seems to have let go of his anger,
and Charles has removed himself from his school

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.650
in order to not let his ego continue to do
damage.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.641
Mystique dies trying to help Jean, which is
less a conclusion to her arc than it is to

00:01:57.641 --> 00:02:01.700
actress Jennifer Lawrence's ongoing attempts
to extricate herself from the X-franchise.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.610
"Here's the X-Men clip, watch, don't watch,
I don't care."

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:05.610
Magneto's newspaper clipping

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.599
While Michael Fassbender's Magneto has come
a long way from 2011's X-Men: First Class,

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:11.080
he hasn't forgotten the past.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:15.670
He might be leading the island nation of Genosha
and acting as a father figure to a sanctuary

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.310
of mutants, but Magneto's got his own lockbox
of secrets, just like Jean.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:24.620
In Erik's case, that lockbox contains his
telepathy-proof helmet and a newspaper clipping

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:28.750
that references his standoff against the X-Men
on the White House lawn in X-Men: Days of

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:29.750
Future Past.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:33.230
Both objects aren't exactly points of pride
for the former mutant terrorist.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.140
The helmet was previously worn by the nazi
scientist who killed Erik's mother, and the

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.030
clipping references the time Magneto was almost
killed by his ex-lover Mystique.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.190
That's some heavy baggage to keep around the
house.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.870
With Dark Phoenix looking to be the end of
the X-Men franchise as we know it, there are

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:51.400
plenty of glances back to the past before
we head off into that wild unknown future.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.580
"And by the way, the women are always saving
the men around here.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.420
You might want to think about changing the
name to X-Women."

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.690
Chris Claremont's cameo

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:02.890
While Stan Lee and Jack Kirby might have created
the original X-Men, the larger X-franchise

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:05.580
owes a huge debt to Chris Claremont.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.319
Claremont is the co-creator of pretty much
all of your favorite X-Men, and he spearheaded

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:12.120
the direction of the X-universe for over 20
years.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.830
From The Dark Phoenix Saga to God Loves, Man
Kills, it's no exaggeration to say that the

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.860
X-Men's popularity as we know it is largely
a product of Claremont's collaborations with

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.110
artists like Dave Cockrum, John Byrne, and
Jim Lee.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:28.170
In terms of the X-Men, Claremont's shadow
looms nearly as large as Stan Lee's own.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:33.100
Fittingly, the filmmakers behind Dark Phoenix
saw fit to give Claremont a cameo in the film.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.739
During the scene in which Charles Xavier is
accepting the accolades for the X-Men's successful

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:40.480
rescue of the Endeavor space ship, Claremont
can be briefly seen in the crowd.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.330
Keep it close to the chess

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:46.659
While the X-Men movie universe has gone through
two timeline shifts and multiple actor recastings,

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:50.200
some things, like Erik and Charles' never-ending
chess game, never change.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.440
By the end of Dark Phoenix, everyone has moved
on; Beast is running the school, Jean Grey's

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:59.099
become an immortal flame bird creature in
the sky, and Charles is hanging out in Paris.

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:02.440
Erik arrives and badgers him into playing
a game of chess as the movie ends.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.830
It's a fitting end to a long and twisting
franchise; while the films had many endings,

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:09.340
Charles and Erik's ongoing chess game remained
a major motif.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.212
It's a nice way to nod to the fact that although
the franchise will be ending as the X-Men

00:04:13.212 --> 00:04:17.329
gets absorbed into Disney and the MCU, some
things will continue on.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:23.830
"I feel a great swell of pity for any poor
fool who comes to that school looking for

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.059
trouble.”

